  "worked_solution": "We can compute the variance of a discrete random variable $X$ using the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute $\\text{E}[X].$ Summing up the products of each value of $X$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =1\\cdot f(1)+5\\cdot f(5)+10\\cdot f(10)+15\\cdot f(15)+20\\cdot f(20) \\\\  & =1(\\frac{5}{15})+5(\\frac{4}{15})+10(\\frac{3}{15})+15(\\frac{2}{15})+20(\\frac{1}{15}) \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}].$ Summing up the products of each value of ${X}^{2}$ and its associated probability, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={1}^{2}\\cdot f(1)+{5}^{2}\\cdot f(5)+{10}^{2}\\cdot f(10)+{15}^{2}\\cdot f(15)+{20}^{2}\\cdot f(20) \\\\  & =1(\\frac{5}{15})+25(\\frac{4}{15})+100(\\frac{3}{15})+225(\\frac{2}{15})+400(\\frac{1}{15}) \\\\  & =\\frac{251}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{251}{3}-{7}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{251}{3}-49 \\\\  & =\\frac{251}{3}-\\frac{147}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{104}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
